---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="File Prep Guide — Cumberland Signworks"
  description="How to prepare artwork files for signs, shirts, vehicle wraps, and print. Vector vs raster, file formats, color modes."
>
  <section class="section bg-cumberland-cream">
    <div class="container-rail max-w-4xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">File prep guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-cumberland-ink">
        Get your files ready. We'll handle the rest.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-relaxed text-cumberland-stone">
        Not sure what to send? Here's the plain-English rundown — no software degree required.
        And if you still don't know, send us whatever you have. We'll tell you if it works.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-rail max-w-4xl space-y-8">

      <!-- Card 1: The short version -->
      <div class="rounded-3xl border border-cumberland-mist bg-cumberland-cream p-8 lg:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow">The short version</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-cumberland-ink">Send a vector file if you have one.</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-cumberland-stone leading-relaxed">
          If your designer gave you an <strong>.AI, .EPS, .SVG, or a PDF exported from Illustrator</strong>,
          send that. Vector files scale to any size without losing sharpness — perfect for signs,
          apparel, and wraps.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-cumberland-stone leading-relaxed">
          If you don't have one, send the highest-resolution file you do have and we'll take a look.
          We'll let you know right away if it'll work for your job.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 2: Vector vs Raster -->
      <div class="rounded-3xl border border-cumberland-mist bg-cumberland-cream p-8 lg:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow">Vector vs. raster</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-cumberland-ink">What's the difference?</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-cumberland-mist p-6">
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-cumberland-forest">Vector</p>
            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-cumberland-stone">
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-clay shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                Math-based — scales to any size perfectly
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-clay shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                Preferred for all print and signage work
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-clay shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                File types: .AI, .EPS, .SVG, .PDF (from Illustrator)
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-cumberland-mist p-6">
            <p class="font-display text-xl text-cumberland-ink">Raster</p>
            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-cumberland-stone">
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-stone shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
                Pixel-based — resolution matters
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-stone shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
                Minimum 300 DPI at the intended print size
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-2">
                <span class="text-cumberland-stone shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
                File types: .PNG, .JPG, .TIFF
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 3: Color mode -->
      <div class="rounded-3xl border border-cumberland-mist bg-cumberland-cream p-8 lg:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow">Color mode</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-cumberland-ink">Which color mode should I use?</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-cumberland-stone">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="font-semibold text-cumberland-ink w-36 shrink-0">Screen printing</span>
            <span>Pantone / PMS colors preferred. Spot color artwork works best.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3 border-t border-cumberland-mist pt-4">
            <span class="font-semibold text-cumberland-ink w-36 shrink-0">Digital print</span>
            <span>CMYK preferred. RGB is OK — we'll convert, but colors may shift slightly.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3 border-t border-cumberland-mist pt-4">
            <span class="font-semibold text-cumberland-ink w-36 shrink-0">Vehicle wraps</span>
            <span>CMYK. Color accuracy matters most here — call us if you need a color match.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 4: Don't have the right file -->
      <div class="rounded-3xl bg-cumberland-forest text-cumberland-cream p-8 lg:p-10">
        <p class="eyebrow text-cumberland-mist">Don't have the right file?</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-cumberland-cream">Send what you have. We'll take it from there.</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-cumberland-mist leading-relaxed">
          You don't have to figure this out on your own. Send us whatever you've got — a photo
          of a business card, a logo from your website, a Word document — and we'll look at it
          and tell you straight whether it'll work, or what we can do to make it work.
        </p>
        <a href="/start-your-project" class="btn-primary mt-6 inline-flex bg-cumberland-clay hover:bg-cumberland-clay/90 border-transparent">
          Start Your Project
        </a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
